=begin pod

=NAME Lling::Llang

=SUBTITLE Composable WFSTs and host-defined automata for Raku

=DESCRIPTION

C<Lling::Llang> provides an eager Unicode/tropical WFST builder, immutable
resource import, lazy composition, and the C<WfstProvider> role for custom Raku
automata. Provider state callbacks are cached and never execute under the
facade's cache lock.

=head1 Core types

=item C<WfstBuilder> constructs a dense immutable graph and is consumed by
C<build>.

=item C<ProviderArc> represents one weighted input/output transition; an
undefined label denotes epsilon.

=item C<ProviderState> represents a complete immutable state expansion.

=item C<WfstProvider> requires C<start-state> and C<state>; C<state-count> may
return the C<Int> type object when unknown.

=head1 Public operations

=item C<abi-version> and C<api-revision> report the loaded native contract.

=item C<WfstBuilder.new(:size-hint)> creates a builder. C<reserve-states>,
C<add-state>, C<set-start>, C<set-final>, C<clear-final>, and C<add-arc> mutate
it. C<build> consumes it on success; C<close> releases it without building and
C<opened> reports whether it remains usable.

=item C<resource($graph)> returns an independent retained resource.
C<import-wfst($resource)> copies a compatible immutable graph into
lling-llang. C<compose($first, $second)> returns a lazy tropical product over
independent snapshots of both inputs.

=item C<provider($implementation, :parallel, :acyclic)> exposes a
C<WfstProvider> through the shared scalar-WFST resource ABI. The optional
C<:unit-domain> and C<:weight-domain> arguments declare its data domains.

=item C<provider-last-error()> copies the latest Raku callback diagnostic for
test and troubleshooting output. It is process-wide and is not a replacement
for calling a provider method directly when structured exception handling is
required.

=head1 Ownership

All returned C<Vinary::Tree::Interop::Wfst> values own one retain. Close them
deterministically. Lazy products retain independent snapshots of their inputs.

=head1 Example

=begin code :lang<raku>
my $builder = WfstBuilder.new;
my $from = $builder.add-state;
my $to = $builder.add-state;
$builder.set-start($from).set-final($to);
$builder.add-arc($from, 'a', 'b', $to);
my $graph = $builder.build;
$graph.close;
=end code

=head1 Provider example

=begin code :lang<raku>
class RewriteAB does WfstProvider {
    method start-state(--> UInt:D) { 0 }
    method state-count(--> Int:D) { 2 }
    method state(UInt:D $id --> ProviderState:D) {
        return ProviderState.new(arcs => [ProviderArc.new(
            input => 'a'.ord, output => 'b'.ord, target => 1,
        )]) if $id == 0;
        return ProviderState.new(final => True, final-weight => 0e0)
            if $id == 1;
        ProviderState.new(valid => False)
    }
}

my $lazy = provider(RewriteAB.new, :acyclic);
$lazy.close;
=end code

=head1 Failure and concurrency contracts

Native failures throw C<X::Lling::Llang> with a stable C<Status>, operation,
and copied diagnostic. Provider exceptions are contained at the C boundary.
Customer C<state> methods execute outside the facade cache lock. Providers are
serialized unless C<:parallel> truthfully declares concurrent reentrancy.

See the packaged C<README.md> and C<t/01-conformance.rakutest> for the complete
provider, composition, error, concurrency, and security contracts.

=end pod
